  <h2 id="angus-mail-provider-module" class="with-back-to-top">Angus Mail provider adapter</h2>
  <nav class="module-capability-links" aria-label="Angus Mail provider adapter capabilities">
    <a href="/email-composition.html#section-bouncing-emails"><span>Envelope</span><strong>Bounce address handling</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="/email-composition.html#section-delivery-status-notification"><span>Request</span><strong>Delivery status notifications</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="/analyzing-send-results.html#section-get-receipt"><span>Inspect</span><strong>Enhanced SMTP responses</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
    <a href="#mail-transport-adapter-spi"><span>Replace</span><strong>Another Jakarta Mail provider</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
  </nav>

  <section><div class="wide">
    <dl class="extension-boundary-map">
      <div><dt>Use the default provider</dt><dd>Depend on <code>simple-java-mail</code>. Its runtime adapter brings
        <a href="https://eclipse-ee4j.github.io/angus-mail/"><code>org.eclipse.angus:angus-mail</code></a> and maps envelope sender,
        DSN and SMTP response handling. The public message, conversion and protection APIs remain provider-neutral.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Import Angus types in your own code</dt><dd>Add <code>org.eclipse.angus:angus-mail</code> as a compile dependency.
        On the module path, also declare <code>requires org.eclipse.angus.mail;</code>. The adapter supplies runtime Angus.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Use another Jakarta Mail implementation</dt><dd>Exclude the bundled adapter and supply the replacement runtime
        implementation. The ordinary <code>Transport</code> API can send standard mail. Provider-specific envelope and DSN features need
        a compatible adapter. See <a href="#mail-transport-adapter-spi">how to add one</a>.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p>Angus is the bundled adapter. MIME and EML conversion also need Jakarta Mail and Activation implementation services.
      If those services are missing, the conversion error points to restoring the default module or adding an implementation.</p>
  </div></section>

  <a href="#mail-transport-adapter-spi" id="mail-transport-adapter-spi" class="section-link h3">&sect;</a>
  <h3>Integrating another Jakarta Mail provider</h3>

  <section>
    <p>
      If your application server already supplies a Jakarta Mail implementation, you can keep it and still use Simple Java Mail.
      For ordinary messages without provider-specific envelope options, Simple Java Mail sends through the provider's standard
      <code class="inline language-java">Transport.sendMessage(...)</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      To swap out Angus, exclude its adapter and make sure your replacement implementation is on the runtime path.
      Sending and MIME conversion both need a provider implementation alongside the Jakarta Mail API.
    </p>
    <details class="doc-disclosure wide full-width-example">
      <summary>Exclude the Angus adapter in Maven or Gradle</summary>
      <p>Maven</p>
<pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;dependency&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;org.simplejavamail&lt;/groupId&gt;
    &lt;artifactId&gt;simple-java-mail&lt;/artifactId&gt;
    &lt;version&gt;{{site.version}}&lt;/version&gt;
    &lt;exclusions&gt;
        &lt;exclusion&gt;
            &lt;groupId&gt;org.simplejavamail&lt;/groupId&gt;
            &lt;artifactId&gt;angus-mail-provider-module&lt;/artifactId&gt;
        &lt;/exclusion&gt;
    &lt;/exclusions&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;</code></pre>
      <p>Gradle</p>
<pre><code class="language-groovy">implementation('org.simplejavamail:simple-java-mail:{{site.version}}') {
    exclude group: 'org.simplejavamail', module: 'angus-mail-provider-module'
}</code></pre>
    </details>
    <div class="wide">
      <h4>Send a signed invoice through your provider</h4>
      <p>Your application needs to submit an invoice from an already signed EML file through its Jakarta Mail provider. Implement
        <code class="inline language-java">MailTransportAdapter</code> to preserve the file byte for byte, carry the requested envelope
        options, and pass back the provider's submission result. The same SPI covers features such as a custom envelope sender or DSNs.</p>

      <dl class="extension-boundary-map">
        <div>
          <dt><strong>Match the provider</strong></dt>
          <dd><p>Implement <code>supports(Transport)</code> for your concrete Transport. Simple Java Mail matches the adapter to the
            actual provider and handles connecting, pooling, and reuse.</p></dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt><strong>Check the prepared mail</strong></dt>
          <dd>
            <p>Declare support for the invoice's <code>PRESERVE_ALL_BYTES</code> requirement with
              <code>supportsContentRequirement(...)</code>. Simple Java Mail also checks the adapter's <code>supportsDeliveryEnvelope(...)</code>
              before dispatch. Inside <code>sendMessage(Transport, PreparedMail)</code>, check the requested options on the already connected
              Transport before starting SMTP submission. See <a href="#mail-transport-adapter-envelope-options">the fixed ENVID walkthrough</a>.</p>
            <details class="doc-disclosure">
              <summary>Content-preservation levels</summary>
              <p>Read <code>PreparedMail.getContentRequirement()</code>. <code>NORMAL</code> is ordinary composed mail.
                <code>PRESERVE_PROTECTED_CONTENT</code> preserves the protected content. <code>PRESERVE_ALL_BYTES</code> preserves every
                supplied byte of an exact message. The deprecated boolean constructor and <code>requiresStableContent()</code>
                remain available for compatibility. Use the enum for all three levels.</p>
            </details>
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt><strong>Submit on the supplied connection</strong></dt>
          <dd><p>Use the content and recipients in <code>PreparedMail</code> and set the requested SMTP envelope separately.
            For this invoice, submit the EML bytes unchanged. Simple Java Mail owns connection setup and routing. If the supplied connection
            is unusable, report the failure using the facts you have.</p></dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt><strong>Return the result</strong></dt>
          <dd>
            <p>Return a non-null <code>MailTransportResult</code>. Simple Java Mail translates it into the
              <a href="analyzing-send-results.html">submission result the caller sees</a>.</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>The server confirms the whole invoice.</strong> Return <code>accepted(...)</code> with the accepted recipients
                and available SMTP reply.</li>
              <li><strong>The final reply goes missing.</strong> Return <code>failedWithUnknownAcceptance(...)</code> with the failure and
                any recipient facts you still know. The relay may already have the invoice. The application decides whether to resend.</li>
            </ul>
            <details class="doc-disclosure">
              <summary>Other provider outcomes</summary>
              <ul>
                <li>Return <code>unknown(...)</code> when the provider returns normally without confirming acceptance.</li>
                <li>Return <code>failed(...)</code> for a Jakarta Mail submission failure. It keeps the original exception and takes
                  recipient groups from the first <code>SendFailedException</code> in the standard next-exception chain, stopping at a
                  loop. Without sufficient recipient details, a 4xx or 5xx reply establishes rejection. Intermediate or unusable codes
                  leave acceptance unknown while retaining the raw reply.</li>
              </ul>
            </details>
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
      <h4 id="mail-transport-adapter-envelope-options">Carry the requested envelope options</h4>
      <p>Say an invoice worker sets a <a href="/email-composition.html#section-dsn-envelope-id">fixed envelope ID</a> with <code>fixingEnvelopeId("invoice-2047-a2")</code> on its
        <code>SmtpDeliveryOptions</code> builder to match a later delivery report to this attempt. Follow that ID through the adapter.</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Opt in to fixed ENVID via <code>supportsDeliveryEnvelope(...)</code>. In <code>sendMessage(...)</code>,
          get the DSN settings from <code>preparedMail.getDeliveryEnvelope().getDeliveryStatusNotification()</code>,
          then read their <code>getEnvelopeId()</code>.</li>
        <li>Check DSN on the actual connected transport. If the connection supports it, issue
          <code>ENVID=invoice-2047-a2</code> on <code>MAIL FROM</code>. Keep the MIME content unchanged.
          If the caller leaves the ID open, you can generate one on a DSN-capable connection or send without one.</li>
        <li>Attach the effective, unencoded ID to the result using <code>result.withEnvelopeId("invoice-2047-a2")</code>,
          including a failed submission that used it. The caller reads it through
          <code>MailSubmissionReceipt.getEnvelopeId()</code>. Leave the ID absent when the send used none or its use is unknown.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Check requested options before <code>MAIL FROM</code>. When the connected transport cannot honor an explicit request,
        throw <code>MailTransportCompatibilityException</code> before starting the transaction so a healthy pooled connection
        remains reusable.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>NOTIFY and RET.</strong> Check support for the requested recipient preferences. The default adapter contract supports the
          existing envelope sender and shared NOTIFY and RET options. Mandatory shared DSN also requires <code>supportsRequiredDeliveryStatusNotification()</code>
          and DSN on this connection.</li>
        <li><strong><a href="/security.html#section-requiretls">REQUIRETLS</a>.</strong> Check authenticated TLS and a usable REQUIRETLS advertisement after negotiation.
          Set <code>withRequireTlsUsed(true)</code> only when you issue the parameter.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Fixed ENVID, recipient-specific NOTIFY and REQUIRETLS each need explicit opt-in through <code>supportsDeliveryEnvelope(...)</code>.</p>
      <details class="doc-disclosure">
        <summary>Apply NOTIFY to each envelope recipient</summary>
        <p><code>DeliveryEnvelope.getRecipientOptions()</code> supplies a <code>DeliveryRecipient</code> for each occurrence.
          Follow the prepared address order through duplicates and provider group expansion.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>An empty recipient-options list means the caller supplied no recipient-specific preferences.</li>
          <li>On an individual recipient, an explicit event set replaces shared NOTIFY. An empty set inherits it.</li>
          <li>If you generate optional ORCPT, use the actual envelope recipient and preserve its notification preferences and the MIME bytes.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>See the <a href="/email-composition.html#section-dsn-recipients">recipient builder example</a>.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="doc-disclosure">
        <summary>Angus behavior and legacy SMTP content</summary>
        <ul>
          <li>The bundled Angus adapter checks DSN after each connection setup. With no fixed ID, it generates a fresh UUID when
            DSN is available and sends without ENVID otherwise.</li>
          <li>The managed Angus command hook adds ORCPT while keeping Angus's recipient loop and reply handling. Ordinary
            transports from caller-owned Angus Sessions lack this hook. Library-owned stock Angus Sessions keep the hook
            with custom socket factories. Those factories still lack physical abort support.</li>
          <li><code>PreparedMail.isLegacySmtpContentSupportEnabled()</code> carries the selected server's compatibility opt-in.
            Existing constructors default it to false, and the content-preservation contract still applies. Managed Angus
            negotiates SMTPUTF8 per message. Caller-owned Sessions, CustomMailer and third-party providers retain their own
            behavior. See the <a href="/sending-and-execution.html#section-international-mail">legacy-content boundaries</a>.</li>
        </ul>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="wide full-width-example">
      <h4>Register the adapter</h4>
      <p>On the classpath, add the implementation to the Java service-provider file.</p>
<pre><code class="language-text small"># META-INF/services/org.simplejavamail.api.mailer.spi.MailTransportAdapter
com.example.ExampleMailTransportAdapter</code></pre>
      <p>On the module path, declare it in <code>module-info.java</code>.</p>
<pre><code class="language-java small">provides org.simplejavamail.api.mailer.spi.MailTransportAdapter
    with com.example.ExampleMailTransportAdapter;</code></pre>
      <p>
        Simple Java Mail selects exactly one adapter whose <code class="inline language-java">supports(...)</code> method matches the concrete Transport.
        Multiple matches fail instead of depending on classpath order.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <h2 id="batch-module" class="with-back-to-top">Batch and clustering support</h2>
  <nav class="module-capability-links" aria-label="Batch module capabilities">
    <a href="/sending-and-execution.html#section-send-execution"><span>Schedule</span><strong>Asynchronous sending</strong><b aria-hidden="true">→</b></a>
